NNAAMMEE
       fsck.minix - check consistency of Minix filesystem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ffsscckk..mmiinniixx [options] _d_e_v_i_c_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ffsscckk..mmiinniixx performs a consistency check for the Linux MINIX filesystem.

       The program assumes the filesystem is quiescent. ffsscckk..mmiinniixx should not
       be used on a mounted device unless you can be sure nobody is writing to
       it. Remember that the kernel can write to device when it searches for
       files.

       The _d_e_v_i_c_e name will usually have the following form:

          allbox tab(:); lt lt. T{

          /dev/hda[1-63] T}:T{

          IDE disk 1 T} T{

          /dev/hdb[1-63] T}:T{

          IDE disk 2 T} T{

          /dev/sda[1-15] T}:T{

          SCSI disk 1 T} T{

          /dev/sdb[1-15] T}:T{

          SCSI disk 2 T}


       If the filesystem was changed, i.e., repaired, then ffsscckk..mmiinniixx will
       print "FILE SYSTEM HAS BEEN CHANGED" and will ssyynncc(2) three times
       before exiting. There is _n_o need to reboot after check.

WWAARRNNIINNGG
       ffsscckk..mmiinniixx should nnoott be used on a mounted filesystem. Using ffsscckk..mmiinniixx
       on a mounted filesystem is very dangerous, due to the possibility that
       deleted files are still in use, and can seriously damage a perfectly
       good filesystem! If you absolutely have to run ffsscckk..mmiinniixx on a mounted
       filesystem, such as the root filesystem, make sure nothing is writing
       to the disk, and that no files are "zombies" waiting for deletion.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ll, ----lliisstt
           List all filenames.

       --rr, ----rreeppaaiirr
           Perform interactive repairs.

       --aa, ----aauuttoo
           Perform automatic repairs. This option implies ----rreeppaaiirr and serves
           to answer all of the questions asked with the default. Note that
           this can be extremely dangerous in the case of extensive filesystem
           damage.

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Be verbose.

       --ss, ----ssuuppeerr
           Output super-block information.

       --mm, ----uunncclleeaarreedd
           Activate MINIX-like "mode not cleared" warnings.

       --ff, ----ffoorrccee
           Force a filesystem check even if the filesystem was marked as
           valid. Marking is done by the kernel when the filesystem is
           unmounted.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       There are numerous diagnostic messages. The ones mentioned here are the
       most commonly seen in normal usage.

       If the device does not exist, ffsscckk..mmiinniixx will print "unable to read
       super block". If the device exists, but is not a MINIX filesystem,
       ffsscckk..mmiinniixx will print "bad magic number in super-block".

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       The exit status returned by ffsscckk..mmiinniixx is the sum of the following:

       00
           No errors

       33
           Filesystem errors corrected, system should be rebooted if
           filesystem was mounted

       44
           Filesystem errors left uncorrected

       77
           Combination of exit statuses 3 and 4

       88
           Operational error

       1166
           Usage or syntax error

